[ system ]
{# ===========================================================================
   Bio Forge - pass two: rewrite one NPC's relationships block.

   Run after a batch finishes, for bios whose ties were composed while some
   neighbours had no profile yet. Those bios could only say that the unwritten
   people were present; now that they exist, the ties are worth asking for
   again.

   Context injected by the DLL:
     actorUUID         (NUMBER)  the subject - REQUIRED, see bioforge_generate
     subjectName       (string)  their display name
     subjectSummary    (string)  their own summary block, as written in pass one
     subjectOccupation (string)  their own occupation block
     currentTies       (string)  the relationships block being replaced
     localActors       (string)  bullet lines, EVERY entry now carrying that
                                 person's own profile - anyone still unwritten
                                 is omitted rather than passed as a blank
     regionDigest      (string)  the settlement reference sheet, may be ""

   Output is ONE `### relationships` section. The DLL splices it into the
   staged bio in place of the old block and touches nothing else, so do not
   emit any other section - there is nowhere for it to go.

   ROLE MARKERS ARE MANDATORY, and every heading needs a guaranteed non-empty
   body. bioforge_generate.prompt documents both, and the rest of the renderer
   quirks, in full.
   =========================================================================== #}
You are revising one section of an existing character profile for a Skyrim roleplay
system: the `relationships` block, and nothing else.

The profile was written before the people around this character had profiles of their
own. It could only note that they were present. Now they have been written, and their
profiles are below. Your job is to make this character's ties to them accurate.

# How to write this

- **Their profiles are authoritative.** They are not guesses and not yours to overrule.
  If someone's line says they are a guest, this character does not treat them as a
  housemate; if it says they are from another town, they are a visitor here. Read what
  each line actually says and let it decide what these two people are to each other -
  a client, a colleague, an employer, a neighbour, a stranger they merely recognise.
- **Do not contradict the subject's own profile below.** Their trade, station and
  temperament are settled; only their ties are being revised.
- **Keep what was already right.** This is a revision, not a fresh start. Ties to gods,
  family, mentors or absent people carry over from the current block unchanged - the new
  information is about the neighbours, and nothing else.
- **Match the two profiles honestly.** Two people in the same trade under one roof are
  colleagues. A person of means staying somewhere and a person who works there are
  customer and provider, in whichever direction the profiles support.
- Seed PUBLIC, ONE-DIRECTIONAL ties only: who they buy from, who takes their money, who
  they answer to, who they keep clear of. Never a mutual bond, shared history, feud or
  romance - the other person's profile will not corroborate it.
- Do not mention anyone marked as travelling with the player. That person is a passing
  stranger here.
- Do not let a powerful name take the section over. If the most important figure in the
  settlement would be a distant fact of life to this person rather than an acquaintance,
  write them that way or leave them out.

# Output format

Output EXACTLY one section, its `###` heading on its own line, and nothing before or
after it. No preamble, no commentary, no markdown fences.

### relationships
100-200 words as bullet points, each starting with "- ", in the form "- Name:
relationship and feeling". Three to five that genuinely matter. Same length and shape as
the block it replaces.

[ end system ]

[ user ]
# The character being revised

Name: {{ subjectName }}

## Their summary
{% if length(subjectSummary) > 0 %}{{ subjectSummary }}
{% else %}(none recorded)
{% endif %}

## Their occupation
{% if length(subjectOccupation) > 0 %}{{ subjectOccupation }}
{% else %}(none recorded)
{% endif %}

## Their relationships as currently written
{# Written before the neighbours below existed. Ties to people NOT in that list were
   composed from this character's own evidence and are still good - carry them over. #}
{% if length(currentTies) > 0 %}{{ currentTies }}
{% else %}(none recorded)
{% endif %}

# The people around them, as they have now been written
{% if length(localActors) > 0 %}{{ localActors }}
{% else %}(nobody nearby has a profile)
{% endif %}
{% if length(regionDigest) > 0 %}
## Who matters in this settlement
{{ regionDigest }}
Background only. Use it where this character's own evidence actually reaches, and not
otherwise.
{% endif %}
{% if length(changedActor) > 0 %}
## What prompted this revision
{{ changedActor }}'s own profile has just been rewritten, and the lines above were written
against the older reading of them. Concentrate on what {{ subjectName }} says about
{{ changedActor }}: make it agree with how {{ changedActor }} is described in the list
above, correcting or dropping anything that no longer fits. Leave every OTHER tie alone —
those were composed from this character's own evidence and are still good.
{% endif %}
Write the revised `### relationships` section for {{ subjectName }} now.
[ end user ]
